# NON-FUNCTIONAL STRAWMAN (imagination-tier; see README.md). The aggregate case:
# zero-argument `dorc why` — a completely different SHAPE from the per-line
# chains: sections, counts, one line per item, every item an address into the
# deeper surface. The organizing ideas: attention-arithmetic up front (what was
# removed, under which license); problems worst-first and CAPPED; trust-spends
# as its own section, never mixed into problems (spent trust is not a problem,
# it is a disclosure); one quantified cheapest-improvement, then stop.

-- book.sh (context) --
The webhost panel: two famous-oracle elisions, one hand-guard lifted, hork
unmodeled at line 10, ufw guarded below it, one sysctl decline at line 6,
one survived elision at line 9 under --risk-faultless-skips.

-- replay --
$ dorc why
web1 · book.sh · probed 4m ago (06:11:52) · plan accepted 06:12:03

  plan: 2 run · 1 verify · 5 removed — of the removals, 4 rest on proof,
  1 on a trusted claim (below).

problems — what dorc could not do for you, worst first:
  1  book.sh:10  hork tune --profile web       runs every apply: nobody has
     described hork, and it walls 2 later sites          (dorc why 10)
  2  book.sh:11  ufw allow 443/tcp             verifies instead of eliding,
     because it sits past the wall at line 10            (dorc why 11)
  3  book.sh:6   sysctl -w vm.drop_caches=3    runs every apply, permanently:
     the author ruled it unanswerable [unsound]          (dorc why 6)

trust spent — removals resting on claims, not proof:
  4  book.sh:9   systemctl enable --now nginx  survived apt-get really
     running, on apt's at-most claim (apt.oracle.sh:82), under your
     --risk-faultless-skips                              (dorc why 9)

the cheapest improvement, quantified: a two-minute convergence oracle for
hork recovers items 1 and 2 whenever hork is converged.
  see:  dorc explain first-wall

(nothing suppressed; on larger books this report caps at the worst 10 —
 `dorc why --all` lifts the cap.)
